{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":482912,"duration_api_ms":219451,"ttft_ms":2050,"num_turns":21,"result":"Iteration complete. Summary:\n\n**Experiment exp004 — `ragCoverageBestOfN`** (best-of-N RAG sets ranked by a deterministic Swift coverage scorer).\n\n- **Hypothesis:** coverage is the wall (budget/who-for/location/timeline/current-state reliably dropped). Sample N full RAG sets at varying temps, then select the best-covering one in Swift — attacking coverage with a deterministic ruler while preserving the natural phrasing of whole model-generated sets.\n- **Result: 0.245 quality (dev) — REGRESSED.** Below best (exp003 = 0.320) and baseline (0.307); spec dropped to 90% (1 error).\n- **Why it failed (honest signal):** A coverage-keyword-only ruler combined with high-temperature sampling backfires. The hotter samples introduced atomicity violations (compound/parenthetical questions) and specificity loss (US tax forms for a German task), and the scorer is blind to those, so it sometimes *selected the worse set*. Coverage itself never moved (still rubric 3).\n- **Takeaway for next time:** selection-by-proxy over the 3B's own samples is insufficient — a useful ruler must score atomicity + specificity + task-fit, not just dimension keyword presence, and sampling needs a low-temp floor. The coverage gap is still the wall; this confirms it's a *capability* limit, not a selection problem.\n\nBuild green ✅, one new run logged ✅ (`results/exp004.json`, `runs.jsonl` run #4), log line prepended to `program.md`. All edits confined to `Sources/DiscoveryAgent/` + `program.md`. Stopping here per the one-experiment rule.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b4f6d8a4-f8c2-404b-b53f-1179ef8a7dd6","total_cost_usd":0.8079705,"usage":{"input_tokens":36,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37084,"cache_read_input_tokens":382513,"output_tokens":15368,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"st